%[text] %[text:anchor:T_176EF8B1] # Transverse-Shear Locking in Shear-Deformable Plates `-` Two Sided Clampled Plate Subject to Vertical Distributed Load
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4745B443] ## Introduction
%[text] This chapter investigates the transverse-shear locking that is exhibited by the low-order Finite Element discretizations of the [Reissner-Mindlin plate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mindlin%E2%80%93Reissner_plate_theory) for the setup of a square plate subject to vertical distributed force $\\bar{p}$ while it is clamped along two of its edges, see the following figure:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![problemSetting_twoSided_SimplySupported_plate.png](text:image:5be2)
%[text] For this problem there is no known analytical solution in terms of the vertical deflection and the cross-sectional rotation fields. As a reference solution it is used the precomputed solution of the same problem using the Kirchhoff plate theory. It is known that for thin plates both the Reissner-Mindlin and Kirchhoff theories should deliver very similar results. However, this should not be the the case for moderetely thick or thick plates. This is demonstrated in this Live Script.
%[text] The Assumed Natural Strain (ANS) method is herein used to alleviate locking. The method entails resampling the strain field at additional evaluation points within each element while assuming a "natural" form of the strain components in the element level before computing the discrete quantities as in the usual case.
%[text] Two types of discretizations are considered herein: A bilinear and a biquadratic quadrilateral discretization. It is first shown the distribution of the transverse shear forces $q\_x$ along the diagonal line shown above and it is highlighted the spurious transverse shear forces, especially for the low-order discretization.
%[text] Subsequently the vertical deflection of the plate at its tip is investigated with respect to the slenderness $L/t$. In this way it is shown, how the spurious transverse shear forces dominate the solution when the bilinear quadrilateral elements are used leading to an ever-decreasing vertical deflection with increasing slenderness.
%[text] Finally a convergence study is performed demonstrating the resulting prohibitly slow convergence of the low-order elements attributed to the transverse shear locking.
%[text] 
%[text] [PREVIOUS Chapter](file:..\4_TransverseShearLocking_ReissnerMindlinCantileverPlate\main_Chapter4_Locking_ReissnerMindlinCantileverPlate.m) *Transverse Shear Locking in Shear-Deformable Plates* *`-`* *Cantilever Plate Subject to Vertical Distributed Load*
%[text] [NEXT Chapter](file:..\..\5_WeakDirichletBoundaryConditions\main_Chapter5_WeakBoundaryConditions_TimoshenkoBeam.m) *Weak Imposition of Dirichlet Boundary Conditions for the Timoshenko Beam Boundary Value Problem*
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E9F6D56E] 
%[text] **Author: Andreas Apostolatos, PhD (aapostol@mathworks.com)**
%[text] **Date: 20.02.2023**
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1ED56119] ## Preamble
clc;
clear;
close all;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5410AC33] ## **Problem setup**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_AC647A48] ### Geometric parameters
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_34929833] Thickness \[m\]
propStr.t = 10^(-1); %[control:slider:6df2]{"position":[17,19]}
%[text] X-coordinate of left corner \[m\]
X0 = 0;
%[text] X-coordinate of right corner \[m\]
XLx = 4;
%[text] Y-coordinate of left corner \[m\]
Y0 = 0;
%[text] Y-coordinate of right corner \[m\]
YLy = XLx;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_578D3F96] ### Material paramerters
%[text] Distributed load \[N/m^2\]
propStr.pBar = -4e4*(propStr.t)^3;
%[text] Distributed moment around x-axis \[N/m\]
propStr.mxBar = 0;
%[text] Distributed moment around y-axis \[N/m\]
propStr.myBar = 0;
%[text] Young's modulus \[N/m^2\]
propStr.E = 1e7;
%[text] Poisson ratio \[dimensionless\]
propStr.nu = 0.3;
%[text] Shear modulus (connected to epsilon\_12 = E/(1+nu)) \[N/m^2\]
propStr.G = propStr.E/2/(1 + propStr.nu);
%[text] Plate's stiffness \[Nm\]
propStr.D = propStr.E*propStr.t^3/12/(1 - propStr.nu^2);
%[text] Shear-correction factor
propStr.alpha = 5/6;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CE11AFBF] ### Newton-Raphson properties for the inversion of the geometry map
%[text] Convergence tolerance
propNewtonRaphson.eps = 1e-9;
%[text] Maximum number of iterations
propNewtonRaphson.maxIt = 10;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4E7AE5CB] ## Definition of the analytical expressions for the two-dimensional basis functions
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E8D9D8B1] ### Define the analytical expressions for the bilinear basis functions
%[text] $N\_1^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(1 - \\xi)(1 - \\eta) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad (1.1) \\\\\nN\_2^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(1 + \\xi)(1 - \\eta) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad (1.2) \\\\\nN\_3^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(1 + \\xi)(1 + \\eta) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad (1.3) \\\\\nN\_4^{Q\_1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(1 - \\xi)(1 + \\eta) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\]. \\quad (1.4)$
syms N1Q1(xi, eta) N2Q1(xi, eta) N3Q1(xi, eta) N4Q1(xi, eta)
N1Q1(xi, eta) = (1 - xi)*(1 - eta)/4;
N2Q1(xi, eta) = (1 + xi)*(1 - eta)/4;
N3Q1(xi, eta) = (1 + xi)*(1 + eta)/4;
N4Q1(xi, eta) = (1 - xi)*(1 + eta)/4;
N_Q1 = [N1Q1 N4Q1 N3Q1 N2Q1];
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9AC71881] ### Define the analytical expressions for the biquadratic basis functions
%[text] $N\_1^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4} \\xi (\\xi - 1) \\eta (1 - \\eta) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad (2.1)  \\\\\nN\_2^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = -\\frac{1}{2}(\\xi + 1)(\\xi - 1)\\eta(\\eta - 1) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\; \\; \\, (2.2) \\\\\nN\_3^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(\\xi + 1)\\xi \\eta(\\eta - 1) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad (2.3) \\\\\nN\_4^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = -\\frac{1}{2}(\\xi + 1)\\xi(\\eta + 1)(\\eta - 1) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\; \\; \\, (2.4) \\\\\nN\_5^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}(\\xi + 1)\\xi(\\eta + 1)\\eta \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad (2.5) \\\\\nN\_6^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = -\\frac{1}{2}(\\xi + 1)(\\xi - 1)(\\eta + 1)\\eta \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\; \\; \\, (2.6) \\\\\nN\_7^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{4}\\xi (\\xi - 1)(\\eta + 1)\\eta \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\],  \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad (2.7) \\\\\nN\_8^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = -\\frac{1}{2}\\xi (\\xi - 1)(\\eta + 1)(\\eta - 1) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \\quad \\; \\; \\, (2.8) \\\\\nN\_9^{Q\_2}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\frac{1}{1}(1 - \\xi^2) (1 - eta^2) \\quad (\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[0, 1\], \n \\quad\\ \\quad \\quad \\quad (2.9)\\\\$
syms N1Q2(xi, eta) N2Q2(xi, eta) N3Q2(xi, eta) N4Q2(xi, eta) ...
    N5Q2(xi, eta) N6Q2(xi, eta) N7Q2(xi, eta) N8Q2(xi, eta) ...
    N9Q2(xi, eta)
N1Q2(xi, eta) = xi*(xi - 1)*eta*(eta - 1)/4;
N2Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*(xi - 1)*eta*(eta - 1)/-2;
N3Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*xi*eta*(eta - 1)/4;
N4Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*xi*(eta + 1)*(eta - 1)/-2;
N5Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*xi*(eta + 1)*eta/4;
N6Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*(xi - 1)*(eta + 1)*eta/-2;
N7Q2(xi, eta) = xi*(xi - 1)*(eta + 1)*eta/4;
N8Q2(xi, eta) = xi*(xi - 1)*(eta + 1)*(eta - 1)/-2;
N9Q2(xi, eta) = (xi + 1)*(xi - 1)*(eta + 1)*(eta - 1)/1;
N_Q2 = [N1Q2 N7Q2 N5Q2 N3Q2 N8Q2 N4Q2 N2Q2 N6Q2 N9Q2];
%[text] ![](text:image:8fba) **Reflect**
%[text] How can the setup of these interpolatory basis functions be generalized?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_652AE3EB] ## **Discretization**
%[text] Both the vertical deflection $w$ and cross-sectional rotation $\\beta\_x$, $\\beta\_y$ fields are discretized using the same basis (shape) functions, namely:
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nw\\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\approx \\sum\_{i=1:N} N\_i \\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\;w\_i ,\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(3\\ldotp 1\\right)\\\\\n\\beta\_x \\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\approx \\sum\_{i=1:N} N\_i \\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\;\\beta\_{x,i} \\;\\ldotp \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(3\\ldotp 2\\right)\\\\\n\\beta\_y \\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\approx \\sum\_{i=1:N} N\_i \\left(\\xi ,\\eta \\right)\\;\\beta\_{y,i} \\;\\ldotp \\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\left(3\\ldotp 3\\right)\n\\end{array}${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Here $N$ indicates the total number of nodes in the mesh. Moreover a Buvnov-Galerkin discretization is used, meaning that both the test and the unknown fields are discretized using the same basis (shape) functions.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D89F4AF8] ### **Quadrilateral mesh**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5501F5BF] #### Choose number of elements in the mesh
%[text] Number of elements along the X-direction
numElx = 5; %[control:slider:8b4f]{"position":[10,11]}
%[text] Number of elements along the Y-direction
numEly = 5; %[control:slider:1fa1]{"position":[10,11]}
%[text] Total number of elements
numEl = numElx*numEly;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_037ECBC4] #### Mesh generation of quadrilateral elements
[nodesX, nodesY] = ...
    generateQuadrilateralMesh(X0, XLx, Y0, YLy, numElx, numEly);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F1FBDA73] ### **Four-noded bilinear quadrilateral mesh**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0B8CC70B] #### Mesh generation
numNodesElQ1 = 4;
mshQ1 = generateBilinearQuadrilateralMesh ...
    (numElx, numEly, numEl, nodesX, nodesY, numNodesElQ1);
numNodes_Q1 = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BC6067B6] #### Set-up of the Dirichlet boundary conditions
numDOFsQ1 = 3*numNodes_Q1;
[freeDOFsQ1, homDOFsQ1, idxX, homDOFsQ1X] = ...
    utilityFunctionsTwoSidedClampedPlate.getFreeDOFsBilinearQuadrilateralMesh ...
    (numNodes_Q1, numElx, numEly);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DB4DD592] #### Mesh plot
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
plotMesh2d(mshQ1, numVertices, true); %[output:073a0e3b]
hold on; %[output:073a0e3b]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ1, ysQ1, zsQ1] = createSupports3D(mshQ1.nodes, homDOFsQ1, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ1(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ1(k, :), ysQ1(k, :), zsQ1(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:073a0e3b]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:073a0e3b]
end
hold off; %[output:073a0e3b]
axis equal; %[output:073a0e3b]
xlabel("$x$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:073a0e3b]
ylabel("$y$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:073a0e3b]
zlabel("$z$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:073a0e3b]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_660B5BB4] #### Display the mesh with the basis functions
isPlotBasisFunctions = false; %[control:checkbox:9d7e]{"position":[24,29]} %[text:anchor:M_0351D2C5]
if isPlotBasisFunctions
    syms xi eta
    NQ1Vis = {(1 - xi)*(1 - eta)/4, (1 - xi)*(1 + eta)/4, ...
        (1 + xi)*(1 + eta)/4, (1 + xi)*(1 - eta)/4};
    [h5, h6, h7] = plotMeshAndBasisFunctions2d(mshQ1, NQ1Vis, true);
end
%[text] ![](text:image:9886) **Try**
%[text] Select the checkbox in this [code section](internal:M_0351D2C5) to create an animation for the bilinear basis functions throughout the mesh. Be aware that this code section can take very long to run due to rendering of complex graphics.
%[text] ![](text:image:03f7) **Reflect**
%[text] What is the support of the bilinear basis functions, namely, the area over which they are not identical zero?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F573047B] ### **Biquadratic quadrilateral mesh**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5438F561] #### Mesh generation
numNodesElQ2 = 9;
[mshQ2, biasX] = generateBiquadraticQuadrilateralMesh ...
    (numElx, numEly, nodesX, nodesY, mshQ1, numNodesElQ1, numNodesElQ2);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_31EC5CC2] #### Set-up of the Dirichlet boundary conditions
numNodesQ2 = numel(mshQ2.nodes(:, 1));
numDOFsQ2 = 3*numNodesQ2;
[freeDOFsQ2, homDOFsQ2] = ...
    utilityFunctionsTwoSidedClampedPlate.getFreeDOFsBiquadraticQuadrilateralMesh ...
    (numNodes_Q1, numNodesQ2, numEly, homDOFsQ1X, idxX, biasX);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1A694615] #### Mesh plot
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
plotMesh2d(mshQ2, numVertices, true); %[output:5ea1e99d]
hold on; %[output:5ea1e99d]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ2, ysQ2, zsQ2] = createSupports3D(mshQ2.nodes, homDOFsQ2, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ2(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ2(k, :), ysQ2(k, :), zsQ2(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:5ea1e99d]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:5ea1e99d]
end
hold off; %[output:5ea1e99d]
axis equal; %[output:5ea1e99d]
xlabel("x") %[output:5ea1e99d]
ylabel("y") %[output:5ea1e99d]
zlabel("z") %[output:5ea1e99d]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F72E52B4] ## Master stiffness matrix and load vector
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_80B68EDF] ### Four-noded bilinear Reissner-Mindlin elements
computeStiffMatrixandForceVectorQ1 = ...
    @computeElementStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlate;
computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivsQ1 = ...
    @computeBilinearBasisFunctionsAndFirstDerivatives;
[KQ1, FQ1] = ...
    computeMasterStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlate ...
    (mshQ1, computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivsQ1, ...
    computeStiffMatrixandForceVectorQ1, propStr);
%[text] Sparsity of the master global stiffness matrix
spy(KQ1) %[output:1abcdf85]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8A82F1B9] ### Nine-noded biquadratic Reissner-Mindlin elements
computeStiffMatrixandForceVectorQ2 = ...
    @computeElementStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlate;
computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivsQ2 = ...
    @computeBiquadraticBasisFunctionsAndFirstDerivatives;
[KQ2, FQ2] = ...
    computeMasterStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlate ...
    (mshQ2, computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivsQ2, ...
    computeStiffMatrixandForceVectorQ2, propStr);
%[text] Sparsity of the master global stiffness matrix
spy(KQ2) %[output:95d998fc]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DA9B705B] #### ![](text:image:633b) **Reflect**
%[text] Why is the sparsity pattern of the master stiffness matrix not aligned around the main-diagonal for the case of the biquadratic quadrilateral elements as compared to the bilinear quadrilateral elements?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4D682A77] ### Four-noded bilinear Reissner-Mindlin elements with Assumed Natural Strain (ANS)
computeStiffMatrixandForceVector_Q1_ANS = ...
    @computeElementStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlateANS;
computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivs_Q1_ANS = ...
    @computeBilinearBasisFunctionsAndFirstDerivatives;
[K_Q1_ANS, F_Q1_ANS] = ...
    computeMasterStiffMatrixandForceVectorReissnerMindlinPlate ...
    (mshQ1, computeBasisFunctionsAndDerivs_Q1_ANS, ...
    computeStiffMatrixandForceVector_Q1_ANS, propStr);
%[text] Sparsity of the master global stiffness matrix
spy(K_Q1_ANS) %[output:2d24a3fe]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_62F5066D] ## Solution of the Reissner-Mindlin plate problem
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7A4BABBD] ### Four-noded bilinear elements
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FAAAD21F] #### Solution of the linear equation system
uQ1 = zeros(numDOFsQ1, 1);
uQ1(freeDOFsQ1, 1) = KQ1(freeDOFsQ1, freeDOFsQ1)\FQ1(freeDOFsQ1);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E935E9FD] #### Visualization of the solution in terms of the vertical displacement field
mshCurrQ1 = mshQ1;
scaling = 1; %[control:slider:6ee3]{"position":[11,12]}
mshCurrQ1.nodes(:, 3) = mshCurrQ1.nodes(:, 3) + ...
    scaling*uQ1(1:3:end, 1);
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
plotMesh2d(mshCurrQ1, numVertices, false); %[output:5519a404]
hold on; %[output:5519a404]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ1, ysQ1, zsQ1] = createSupports3D(mshCurrQ1.nodes, homDOFsQ1, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ1(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ1(k, :), ysQ1(k, :), zsQ1(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:5519a404]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:5519a404]
end
hold off; %[output:5519a404]
axis equal; %[output:5519a404]
xlabel("$x$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:5519a404]
ylabel("$y$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:5519a404]
zlabel("$z$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:5519a404]
title("Plate's deformation using Q1 elements") %[output:5519a404]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_67D51DE7] #### Visualization of the solution in terms of the rotational field
mshCurrQ1Betay = mshQ1;
scaling = 100; %[control:slider:1455]{"position":[11,14]}
mshCurrQ1Betay.nodes(:, 3) = mshCurrQ1Betay.nodes(:, 3) + ...
    scaling*uQ1(2:3:end);
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
plotMesh2d(mshCurrQ1Betay, numVertices, false); %[output:5b62c9fe]
hold on; %[output:5b62c9fe]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ1, ysQ1, zsQ1] = createSupports3D(mshCurrQ1Betay.nodes, homDOFsQ1, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ1(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ1(k, :), ysQ1(k, :), zsQ1(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:5b62c9fe]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:5b62c9fe]
end
hold off; %[output:5b62c9fe]
axis equal; %[output:5b62c9fe]
xlabel("x") %[output:5b62c9fe]
ylabel("y") %[output:5b62c9fe]
zlabel("$\beta_y$", Interpreter="latex") %[output:5b62c9fe]
title("Distribution of rotation field $\beta_x$ using Q1 elements", ... %[output:group:3503773e] %[output:5b62c9fe]
    Interpreter="latex") %[output:group:3503773e] %[output:5b62c9fe]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7FCFA141] ### Nine-noded biquadratic Reissner-Mindlin elements
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F68020F1] #### Solution of the linear equation system
uQ2 = zeros(numDOFsQ2, 1);
uQ2(freeDOFsQ2, 1) = KQ2(freeDOFsQ2, freeDOFsQ2)\FQ2(freeDOFsQ2);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_662D90AA] #### Visualization of the solution in terms of the vertical displacement field
mshCurrQ2 = mshQ2;
scaling = 1; %[control:slider:39f7]{"position":[11,12]}
mshCurrQ2.nodes(:, 3) = mshCurrQ2.nodes(:, 3) + ...
    scaling*uQ2(1:3:end, 1);
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
plotMesh2d(mshCurrQ2, numVertices, false); %[output:602f0c61]
hold on; %[output:602f0c61]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ2, ysQ2, zsQ2] = createSupports3D(mshCurrQ2.nodes, homDOFsQ2, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ2(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ2(k, :), ysQ2(k, :), zsQ2(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:602f0c61]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:602f0c61]
end
hold off; %[output:602f0c61]
axis equal; %[output:602f0c61]
xlabel("$x$", "Interpreter", "latex") %[output:602f0c61]
ylabel("$y$", "Interpreter", "latex") %[output:602f0c61]
zlabel("$z$", "Interpreter", "latex") %[output:602f0c61]
title("Plate's deformation using the Q2 elements") %[output:602f0c61]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_79A99FD4] #### Visualization of the solution in terms of the rotational field
mshCurrQ2Betay = mshQ2;
scaling = 1; %[control:slider:0637]{"position":[11,12]}
mshCurrQ2Betay.nodes(:, 3) = mshCurrQ2Betay.nodes(:, 3) + ...
    scaling*uQ2(2:3:end, 1);
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
plotMesh2d(mshCurrQ2Betay, numVertices, false); %[output:3f4fa783]
hold on; %[output:3f4fa783]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ2, ysQ2, zsQ2] = createSupports3D(mshCurrQ2Betay.nodes, homDOFsQ2, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ2(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ2(k, :), ysQ2(k, :), zsQ2(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:3f4fa783]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:3f4fa783]
end
hold off; %[output:3f4fa783]
axis equal; %[output:3f4fa783]
xlabel("$x$", "Interpreter", "latex") %[output:3f4fa783]
ylabel("$y$", "Interpreter", "latex") %[output:3f4fa783]
zlabel("$z$", "Interpreter", "latex") %[output:3f4fa783]
title("Distribution of rotation field $\beta_x$ using Q1 elements", ... %[output:group:6faecc52] %[output:3f4fa783]
    Interpreter="latex") %[output:group:6faecc52] %[output:3f4fa783]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CD51BFF9] ### Four-noded bilinear Reissner-Mindlin elements with Assumed Natural Strain (ANS)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_239AFEE9] #### Solution of the linear equation system
uQ1ANS = zeros(numDOFsQ1, 1);
uQ1ANS(freeDOFsQ1, 1) = ...
    K_Q1_ANS(freeDOFsQ1, freeDOFsQ1)\F_Q1_ANS(freeDOFsQ1);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A488E2D5] #### Visualization of the solution in terms of the vertical displacement field
mshCurrQ1ANS = mshQ1;
scaling = 1; %[control:slider:5676]{"position":[11,12]}
mshCurrQ1ANS.nodes(:, 3) = mshCurrQ1ANS.nodes(:, 3) + ...
    scaling*uQ1ANS(1:3:end, 1);
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
plotMesh2d(mshCurrQ1ANS, numVertices, false); %[output:98d19f85]
hold on; %[output:98d19f85]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ1ANS, ysQ1ANS, zsQ1ANS] = ...
    createSupports3D(mshCurrQ1ANS.nodes, homDOFsQ1, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ1(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ1ANS(k, :), ysQ1ANS(k, :), zsQ1ANS(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:98d19f85]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:98d19f85]
end
hold off; %[output:98d19f85]
axis equal; %[output:98d19f85]
xlabel("$x$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:98d19f85]
ylabel("$y$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:98d19f85]
zlabel("$z$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:98d19f85]
title("Plate's deformation using Q1 (ANS) elements") %[output:98d19f85]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_81F289A7] #### Visualization of the solution in terms of the rotational field
mshCurrQ1ANSBetay = mshQ1;
scaling = 1; %[control:slider:9ceb]{"position":[11,12]}
mshCurrQ1ANSBetay.nodes(:, 3) = mshCurrQ1ANSBetay.nodes(:, 3) + ...
    scaling*uQ1ANS(2:3:end, 1);
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
plotMesh2d(mshCurrQ1ANSBetay, numVertices, false); %[output:11aa513a]
hold on; %[output:11aa513a]
scalVer = 1;
[xsQ1ANS, ysQ1ANS, zsQ1ANS] = ... 
    createSupports3D(mshCurrQ1ANSBetay.nodes, homDOFsQ1, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ1(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ1ANS(k, :), ysQ1ANS(k, :), zsQ1ANS(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, ... %[output:11aa513a]
        'Color', 'black'); %[output:11aa513a]
end
hold off; %[output:11aa513a]
axis equal; %[output:11aa513a]
xlabel("$x$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:11aa513a]
ylabel("$y$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:11aa513a]
zlabel("$z$", "Interpreter","latex") %[output:11aa513a]
title("Distribution of rotation field $\beta_x$ using Q1 (ANS) elements", ... %[output:group:4d020446] %[output:11aa513a]
    Interpreter="latex") %[output:group:4d020446] %[output:11aa513a]
